Subject: [PATCH] xma: a restarted voice puts its decoder back instead of reopening it

A freed or cleared XMA context reopened its FFmpeg decoder on the next decode
(the cached format was invalidated), so every restart rebuilt the decoder's
five MDCTs, their sine tables and seven VLC tables: on the owner's PC that was
about 12% of the FMOD sound thread's time, twice its real decoding.

The decoder's private data is kept as it was just after avcodec_open2 (its
non-zero byte runs) and put back byte for byte at a restart, after
avcodec_flush_buffers. Its pointers are the same allocations of the same open
context and decoding only reads them, so the decoder starts exactly as a
reopened one would. For the first 24 restarts of a session a reopened decoder
decodes the same packets beside the restored one (up to 8 each) and the frames
are compared bit for bit; a difference logs an error, gives the game the
reopened decoder's frame and decoder, and makes every later restart reopen as
before. xma_restart_without_reopen (Audio, on) switches it off.

